## 3. Supplementary and Complementary Legislation

Some constitutional provisions establish a framework but leave details to Parliament.

Parliament can fill these gaps through ordinary legislation. This is another feature supporting the flexibility of the Constitution.

### A. Citizenship — Articles 5 to 11

- Part II of the Constitution deals primarily with citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution.
- It does not comprehensively prescribe all the ways citizenship can be acquired or terminated thereafter.
- Article 11 empowers Parliament to make laws concerning citizenship.

**Example:** The Citizenship Act, 1955 provides for citizenship by birth, descent, registration, naturalisation and incorporation of territory. It also addresses termination of citizenship.  
[Source: Ministry of Home Affairs](https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/2026-01/Citizenship_Act_1955_20012026.pdf)

### B. Untouchability — Article 17

- Article 17 abolishes untouchability and prohibits its practice.
- It declares that enforcement of any disability arising out of untouchability is an offence punishable by law.
- Parliament provides the details through legislation.

**Example:** The Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 specifies offences and penalties relating to the practice of untouchability.

### C. Elections

The Constitution provides the broad framework for elections, while legislation provides many operational details.

**Example:** The Representation of the People Acts, 1950 and 1951 regulate various aspects of elections.

**Important correction:** The constitutional provisions concerning elections, including Articles 324–329, cannot simply be changed by ordinary law. The Representation of the People Acts supplement the constitutional framework; they do not amend the Constitution.

